\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Proposition}
\title{The projected normal recurrence}
\date{2026-11-27}

\p{For the \vocabk{normal transfer}{fdip-01BF} decompose the
value on layer #{l} as #{\psi_l=u_l+v_l} in #{W_+\oplus W_-}.
The kernel equation there is
##{B_{+,l}u_l+tE_lv_l=u_{l-1},\qquad
v_{l+1}=B_{-,l}v_l-tE_l^\dagger u_l.}
It determines a unique output #{(u_l,v_{l+1})} for every input
#{(u_{l-1},v_l)} exactly when #{B_{+,l}} is invertible.
In that case the step is #{T_l}. If all steps exist, the
kernel of the free-ended slab is isomorphic via #{v_1} to
##{\ker\left(P_- T_L\cdots T_1\big|_{W_-}\right).}}

\tikzfigs{0.76}{\begin{tikzpicture}[
 pair/.style={draw,rounded corners,inner sep=5pt},
 slice advance/.style={-{Stealth[length=2mm]},thick}
]
\node[pair] (input) at (0,0) {$(u_{l-1},v_l)$};
\node[pair] (output) at (4,0) {$(u_l,v_{l+1})$};
\draw[slice advance] (input) to node[above] {$T_l$} (output);
\end{tikzpicture}}
\p{At the bottom the first input has #{u_0=0}.
At the top the last output must have #{v_{L+1}=0}.}

\proof{
\p{In the common frame the two normal hops of the Wilson
kernel are #{-P_+\psi_{l-1}/t} and #{-P_-\psi_{l+1}/t}.
The normal Wilson term adds #{\psi_l/t} on the same layer.
Thus #{tK_{L,U}\psi} there is
#{(\bbI+tA_l+tD_l)\psi_l-u_{l-1}-v_{l+1}}.
The centered term is skew-adjoint and exchanges the normal
ranges, so its component from #{W_+} to #{W_-} is
#{-E_l^\dagger}. Projection gives the two equations.
Solving first for #{u_l} gives the displayed step.
If #{0\ne u_l\in\ker B_{+,l}}, both this value and zero
give solutions at zero input, with their respective #{v_{l+1}}.}

\p{The absent end hops give exactly the two end conditions.
Starting at #{(0,v_1)} and following the steps recovers every
layer uniquely. The last condition is the vanishing of the
#{W_-} projection of #{T_L\cdots T_1(0,v_1)}.}
}
